LANSING, MI -- Attorney General Bill Schuette asked lawmakers for more than $10 million to both investigate and defend the state over the Flint water crisis, according to a document obtained by MLive.
In total the budget sends $6.9 million over two fiscal years to the Department of the Attorney General. That money is to pay for both sides of the crisis -- the department is working to defend the state against incoming lawsuits but hired outside legal counsel to prosecute over the crisis.
But in a May memo to Senate Majority Leader Arlan Meekhof obtained through the Freedom of Information Act, Schuette asked for $10.28 million in funding for Flint water crisis legal costs.
Read the full memo here.
Of that, he wanted $5.7 million to go toward a Flint water investigation he announced in January that is spearheaded by private attorney Todd Flood. So far the AG's investigation has resulted in three criminal and two civil cases. Lawmakers ended up appropriating $3.9 million for the investigation.
Schuette spokeswoman Andrea Bitely said the investigation was proceeding within that budget.
"The investigation into the Flint water crisis is going forward, as the Attorney General says 'without fear or favor' and will be accomplished fully and thoroughly with the resources we have been allocated by the Legislature," Bitely said.
AG Schuette's Flint investigation funding upped to $4.9M by state board
Schuette's office is also working the other side of the issue. State employees at the Attorney General's office are working to defend the state. There is a "conflict wall" between the work the AG is doing to prosecute in relation to the water crisis and the work other state attorneys are doing to defend the state against water crisis claims.
Bitely said there was no overlap between the sides of the wall, electronically or verbally.
According to information Schuette included in the memo to Meekhof, R-West Olive, attorneys in his office were defending the state in 84 cases related to bodily injury due to lead exposure, lost property value, damage to infrastructure and death and illness associated with Legionella.
Schuette anticipated that more than 100 cases had yet to be filed in the May memo, and a spokesperson said Thursday that more than 530 notices of intent to sue had been filed.
Schuette asked for $4.6 million to go to the Department of the Attorney General to defend the state against incoming lawsuits. Lawmakers appropriated $3 million.
Funding for the Flint water investigation got off to a rocky start. When Schuette initially launched the investigation key lawmakers signaled they wanted him to work within his existing budget.
