Close followers of African politics would be forgiven for experiencing deja vu upon hearing of the peace agreement between Sudan and South Sudan.
Aimed at providing a comprehensive solution to the full range of disputes that has repeatedly put the newly divorced countries at risk of war, the final treaty makes the same mistake as its unfortunately named predecessor – the Comprehensive Peace Agreement of 2005. Until all the outstanding issues from these two agreements are resolved, the people of both countries can expect more of the same – ongoing conflict, ethnic cleansing and humanitarian disaster.
Since the agreement was signed last Thursday international leaders – from President Obama to Lady Ashton of the EU – have been tripping over themselves to praise it. Human rights groups, peace activists and non-government figures in Sudan and South Sudan have been left asking why.
Despite being hailed as "a giant step forward for both countries" by African Union mediator Thabo Mbeki, the only significant result is that the two countries have agreed to solve the oil issue. Production of oil in South Sudan and its transportation to the only available port in its northern neighbour will resume. But this is simply a case of mutual economic self-interest being satisfied.
Although the two presidents agreed on a demilitarised zone and principles of border demarcation which in itself is good news, little real progress has been made to address the problems of disputed border areas such as Abyei. The fate of Blue Nile and Southern Kordofan – two regions left in the north that are waging an insurgency against Khartoum – was also not addressed.
The CPA previously left these crucial issues unresolved – and the people of both countries have been suffering the consequences ever since the south declared independence last July.
Border uncertainty has allowed Sudan to continue pursuing ethnic cleansing in the Blue Nile and Southern Kordofan regions, in pursuit of a racially pure Arab state. Hundreds of thousands of civilians have been forced from their homes and killed.
These actions have led to an unfolding humanitarian refugee crisis in South Sudan as civilians flee what has been described as the worst war crimes since Darfur. As if this situation was not bad enough, only last week the UN confirmed sighting of a plane coming from Sudan that was dropping arms to rebels in South Sudan.
The result of failing to address these issues has been clear for over a year now. Seven years on from the CPA and it seems little has been learnt. With both sides avoiding resolving the so-called "security file", history will simply repeat itself. Unarmed civilians, rather than leaders and politicians, will as ever pay the price.
A truly comprehensive peace settlement is well overdue. Rather than praising a clearly inadequate agreement it is time the international community put pressure on both sides to resolve their differences – from finalising the border between the two countries to holding the long delayed referendum in Abyei on whether its residents wish to be part of the north or south.
