MUSKEGON, MI -- For 30 years, Hazel Raddatz has braved the elements for the people on her mail route.
Through the proverbial ice and snow, the sleet, the heat and storms and every human condition in between, she's delivered people's mail — and in a most unassuming and uncanny way, she's tied us together. She helped us be a neighborhood, a community; not just so many houses in a row on a street.
I say "us" because Hazel was my mail carrier when I lived in Lakeside, an area with lots of sidewalks and no mail boxes at the curbs. Mail — and plenty of it in those days — arrived at our front doors, long before social media changed our means of communication. I was at work most days when the mail arrived, and honestly, I didn't know who put it there.
Then one day, I found a note tucked in with the rest of the mail in the mailbox.
"Pretty flowers," it said. "I love your window boxes. Hazel."
Well, I had to see who this was, who would take the time to write a note to a customer on a mail route. So the next time I was home and heard the mail truck rumble to a stop, I went outside to say hello.
There she was, this woman with a smile like sunshine, greeting everyone on the block as if we were her best friends, walking along and saying hello, giving a quick wave to those looking out the window for her.
I mean, she was something else again.
"I like people," she says. "I'm in their lives every day, and well, I like to make connections with them; to find some way to connect some how."
And in doing so, she connected us to one another.
"Has Hazel been here yet?" we'd ask.
Not: "The mail here?"
But the person who brought it to us.
Seven years ago, Hazel took that incredible gift of hospitality with her to a new route — which just so happened to be in the Lincoln School neighborhood where I grew up in Norton Shores.
