A Portland nonprofit that helps people with disabilities to live independently in their homes is preparing to break ground on a home of its own.
Community Vision Inc. is planning a four-story office building at 1949 S.E. Division St., at the southeast corner of Ladd's Addition. The $6.5 million Seven Corners Community Collaborative, as Community Vision is calling the project, will allow the organization to build a headquarters it says will be a model for universal accessibility. It will also be designed to provide space for like-minded nonprofits.
The project grew out of Community Vision's model of renovating tax-foreclosed properties to be accessible and affordable for people with disabilities. The Ladd's Addition site had been foreclosed upon in the late 1990s and remained vacant until Multnomah County gave it to the nonprofit last year.
Community Vision, meanwhile, was looking to buy a new headquarters. Currently in offices on Southwest Skyline Boulevard in Portland, it wanted to move closer to most of its constituents and to an area more accessible by public transit.
"We had essentially given up on trying to find the right building, just because of the cost and availability," said Executive Director Joe Wykowski. "This gave us an opportunity to revive our efforts. It was a perfect match for us."
By building its headquarters from the ground up, Wykowski said, Community Vision will be able to showcase techniques for accessible construction. Rooms and corridors will be oriented and painted so people with impaired sight will be able to navigate more easily, and the building will be easy to negotiate in a wheelchair.
