Throughout the entire search for 83-year-old Nivia Benintendi of Margate, I imagine a lot of people were giving extra thought to their own mothers.
Perhaps that's why the three-week-long search stayed in the news so long. Benintendi simply seemed like everybody's South Florida mother.
Benintendi -- who apparently drowned when her car went into a canal while she was returning home from a bingo game -- used to go with her friends to Morrison's Cafeteria, we were told.
She used to go shopping in the warehouse district on 19th Street. She used to play bingo several nights a week.
And, as a vibrant woman, she enjoyed the independence she got from having a driver's license -- even though her children and some of her neighbors were not overjoyed with her driving skills.
All of which has made me believe that seniors would want to take periodic road tests for driver's licenses, rather than always fighting against such a law.
REFLEXES NOT AS SHARP
We'll probably never know what it was that caused Benintendi to drive into that canal.
We do know, however, that she wasn't a good driver.
Her independence was important to her, and I can understand that. Like Benintendi, my mother is a widow living in South Florida. Like many women in her situation, she is independent and active, and would be totally lost without access to her car.
But I still think seniors -- when they reach the age of 70 or 75 -- should be required to take a road test every couple of years before renewing a driver's license.
I know, I know.
That yelling in the background is from all the seniors, claiming they haven't had a ticket in 47 years and young people are just as hazardous on the road and that making seniors take a driver's test is vicious discrimination.
Of course, if you read an interesting series in this very paper recently, you know the numbers show that seniors have a good deal of difficulties to overcome on the road.
Drivers older than 75 have the highest rate of eyesight, hearing and seizure problems, we were informed. And drivers 75 and older are twice as likely as any other age group to die in an automobile accident.
"The group that does the poorest on the (driver's) test is 75 and over -- for obvious reasons," Don Keirn, chief of the Florida Driver Improvement Bureau, said two years ago.
"Their response time isn't as good; their vision isn't as good; their retention isn't as good."
So it isn't merely a question of driving ability. It is a matter of reflexes and the aging process. A younger person simply reacts quicker at the wheel than somebody in their 70s. Such is life.
